# AI working state

Globally-useful state only. Detail lives in `state/<track>.md` — see the
index below. Read this file first every session; read a track's state file
only when you are touching that track.

## Latest

Fan-out, second iteration. `SeqRing` (`internal/broadcast/seqring.go`) is
`Ring` with the lock taken off the read path — a pointer per slot so the
writer never mutates what a reader holds (RCU-shaped, GC as the grace
period), and a seqlock validated once per batch instead of per message, so
a `Recv` is atomic loads with no read-modify-write anywhere. `channel.go`
builds these now. Paced fan-out is 8.15 ns/msg against Ring's 17.50 at a
thousand subscribers and 8.22 against 15.12 at ten thousand (medians of
eight); churn under load is 219ns against 586 with one sender and 236ns
against 1476 with four, and **flat in the sender count** because
`Subscribe` takes no lock to queue behind. At the wire a busy room (700
conns, 195k deliveries/s) improves mean and tail on both interleaved
rounds; a quiet room is a wash, which is the design being honest — what it
removes is reader contention, and a room where every write pump sleeps
between messages has none. Costs 28ns against 8ns on the send path and one
24-byte allocation per broadcast, which the indirection needs to stay
race-free. Detail, the regimes and three new harness gotchas in
`state/broadcast.md`.

Before that: CI. `.woodpecker.yaml` runs `make test-race`, `make lint` and the
production build on every push and pull request, and `make vulncheck` on
a cron; `make init` sets a clone up (linters + the git pre-commit hook)
and `make lint` now refuses to run without the tools instead of dying
halfway through. Getting there meant clearing the 19 findings `make lint`
had accumulated, none of them a bug. Every Go cache lives on the agent's
persistent `/cache` volume, the same convention as `kikapcsologo/backend`.
`make update-deps` moved no module — they were all current — but
govulncheck caught the toolchain pin at go1.26.4 with a `crypto/tls` CVE
fixed in go1.26.5, so the pin is bumped and `update-deps` now updates it
too. Detail, the step table and the rough edges — still on the v1
golangci-lint module path, no cron created yet — in `state/ci.md`.

Before that: the load generator. `cmd/loadgen` is a separate process holding N
connections open, a configurable percentage of which talk, reporting
delivery ratio, latency percentiles, refusals by `ERR` code and
disconnections by close reason. Mechanics in `internal/loadgen`, detail
and measurements in `state/loadgen.md`.

First numbers on one laptop with the server beside it: 500 connections
with 10% speaking is 47k deliveries/s at 99.8% and p99 20ms; 1500
connections with 20% speaking at 20/s is where the server starts dropping
people for lag, which the report names (`StatusPolicyViolation: too
slow`). Anything past ~100k deliveries/s on one box is measuring the box.

Profiling under that load found one thing worth fixing, and it is fixed:
`conn.write` built a `context.WithTimeout` per delivered frame, which was
99% of everything the server allocated. A write pump now drains its whole
batch under one deadline (`conn.writeBatch`), so `WriteTimeout` bounds a
wakeup rather than a single frame — the stricter reading where it matters.
At a quiet 49k deliveries/s it changes nothing, because a batch is one
frame; at 3000 connections with 90% speaking it takes `WithTimeout` from
11% of CPU to 1.7% and garbage from 9.95GB per 20s to 1.24GB. Numbers in
`state/loadgen.md`.

